antic Ocean and the Mediterranean Sea."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-round-sardinella"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Round_sardinella"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Lekor","dish/lekor"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Rastrelliger kanagurta","ingredient/rastrelliger-kanagurta"],["Sago","ingredient/sago"],["Silver-stripe round herring","ingredient/silver-stripe-round-herring"],["Tapioca starch","ingredient/tapioca-starch"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a1/Sardinella_aurita_New_Jersey.jpg/500px-Sardinella_aurita_New_Jersey.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"avnj","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sardinella_aurita_New_Jersey.jpg"}},"ingredient/ayam-kampong":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ayam-kampong","n":"Ayam Kampong","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The Ayam Kampong (older spelling) or Ayam Kampung is a chicken breed from Indonesia and Malaysia. The name means simply \"free-range chicken\" or literally \"village chicken\". In Indonesia and Malaysia, the term ayam kampung refer to indigenous chickens that are raised using traditional free range production techniques by almost every household in the village. It is a diverse population which resulted from the uncontrolled cross-breeding of red jungle-fowl, indigenous Southeast Asian chickens and exotic chickens of various types imported in the late 1800s by European, mainly Dutch and British, settlers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ayam-kampong"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayam_Kampong"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Lime","ingredient/lime"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/96/Ayam_kampung_jantan.jpg/500px-Ayam_kampung_jantan.jpg","w":500,"h":411,"by":"Zamwan","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayam_kampung_jantan.jpg"}},"ingredient/lens":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lens","n":"Lens","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Vicia is a genus of over 240 species of flowering plants that are part of the legume family (Fabaceae), and which are commonly known as vetches. Vicia species are native to Eurasia, Africa, and the Americas."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lens"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lens_(plant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Cypriot cuisine","cuisine/cypriot-cuisine",[["Mujaddara","dish/mujaddara"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Carum","ingredient/carum"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Lentils","ingredient/lentils"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/53/Vicia_orobus1_eF.jpg/500px-Vicia_orobus1_eF.jpg","w":500,"h":719,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicia_orobus1_eF.jpg"}},"ingredient/tortilla-chip":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tortilla-chip","n":"Tortilla chip","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A tortilla chip is a snack food made from corn tortilla, which are cut into triangles and then fried or baked (alternatively they may be discs pressed out of corn masa then fried or baked). Corn tortillas are made of nixtamalized corn, vegetable oil, salt and water. Although first mass-produced commercially in the U.S. in Los Angeles in the late 1940s, tortilla chips grew out of Mexican cuisine, where similar items were well known, such as totopos and tostadas."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tortilla-chip"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tortilla_chip"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Nachos","dish/nachos"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Cheese sauce","ingredient/cheese-sauce"],["Tortilla chips","ingredient/tortilla-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e9/4003_-_Zermatt_-_Restaurant_Weisshorn.JPG/500px-4003_-_Zermatt_-_Restaurant_Weisshorn.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Andrew Bossi","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4003_-_Zermatt_-_Restaurant_Weisshorn.JPG"}},"ingredient/budu-sauce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/budu-sauce","n":"Budu (sauce)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Budu is a traditional anchovy sauce condiment made among ethnic Malays populating Thai provinces Yala, Narathiwat and Pattani plus Malaysian states of Kelantan and Terengganu altogether east coast of the Malay Peninsula, to the Natuna Islands (called pedek or pedok), South Sumatra, Bangka Island, and West Kalimantan (called rusip) in Borneo of Indonesia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-budu-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budu_(sauce)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Nasi kerabu","dish/nasi-kerabu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Clitoria ternatea","ingredient/clitoria-ternatea"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1d/YosriBuduMempelam.jpg/500px-YosriBuduMempelam.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Yosri","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YosriBuduMempelam.jpg"}},"ingredient/roe":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/roe","n":"Roe","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Roe, (  ROH) or hard roe, is the fully ripe internal egg masses in the ovaries, or the released external egg masses, of fish and certain marine animals such as shrimp, scallop, sea urchins and squid. As a seafood, roe is used both as a cooked ingredient in many dishes, and as a raw ingredient for delicacies such as caviar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-roe"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roe"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine",[["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"]]],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine",[["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Carp","ingredient/carp"],["Cod","ingredient/cod"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/ff/Caviar_spoons.jpg/500px-Caviar_spoons.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"THOR","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caviar_spoons.jpg"}},"dish/the-peak-lookout":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/the-peak-lookout","n":"The Peak Lookout","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Fusion cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["The Peak Lookout is a restaurant located in a heritage house at Victoria Gap, near the summit of Victoria Peak on Hong Kong Island, Hong Kong. It is housed in a 19th-century Grade II Historic Building. Originally known as the Old Peak Café, the restaurant has an open terrace overlooking Aberdeen, Pok Fu Lam Country Park and the South China Sea. The Peak Lookout at Terminal 1 of the Hong Kong International Airport opened in November 2012."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-the-peak-lookout"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Peak_Lookout"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Fusion cuisine","links":[["Blok's Restaurant","dish/blok-s-restaurant"],["Karen Keygnaert","dish/karen-keygnaert"],["Kojacha","dish/kojacha"],["Saucebox","dish/saucebox"],["Variety Jones (restaurant)","dish/variety-jones-restaurant"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/53/HK_Peak_Cafe_200908.jpg/500px-HK_Peak_Cafe_200908.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"WiNG","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HK_Peak_Cafe_200908.jpg"}},"ingredient/urab":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/urab","n":"Urab","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Urap, oerap, krawu, urab or in its plural form urap-urap is a salad dish of steamed or boiled vegetables mixed with seasoned and spiced grated coconut for dressing. It is commonly found in Indonesian cuisine, more precisely Javanese cuisine. Urap can be consumed on its own as a salad for vegetarian meals or as a side dish. Urap is usually found as a prerequisite side dish of Javanese tumpeng, a cone-shaped rice mound surrounded with assorted dishes, as well as part of a nasi kuning dish. In Balinese cuisine, it is known as urab sayur."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-urab"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urab"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Tumpeng","dish/tumpeng"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fried chicken","ingredient/fried-chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d7/Urap.JPG/500px-Urap.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urap.JPG"}},"dish/walnut-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/walnut-soup","n":"Walnut soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Walnut soup is a broth-based or cream-based soup prepared using walnuts as a main ingredient. It is sometimes prepared in combinations using other ingredients, such as pumpkin and walnut soup. Walnut soup is a part of the cuisines of China, Italy and Mexico."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-walnut-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","ref":"site/thewoksoflif